Open follow-ups (from latest compare report 2026-05-09)
=======================================================

- [LIKELY-RESOLVED 2026-05-09; needs clean-data confirm] All four
  follow-ups below were captured BEFORE the Class B USE-leak fix
  (162f2fde) and the ACCEPT_KEYWORDS fix (29c1c223). Targeted
  pretend re-runs after both fixes show plans collapsed by ~6-15x:
  app-misc/mc 41->6 actions, net-irc/irssi 3 actions (no perl
  pull-in, no -r2 mismatch), net-dns/dnsmasq 7 actions (was +3),
  media-libs/freetype 6 actions (was +9), www-servers/nginx
  OK(assumed) with 9 actions (was failing with assumptions). The
  perl/python over-pull cluster shared a root cause with the alsa
  cluster: the hardcoded fallback USE included PYTHON_TARGETS and
  PERL_FEATURES values that emerge never sees on a clean baseline.
  The irssi version mismatch (1.4.5-r2 vs 1.4.5) is a Class C
  symptom resolved by ACCEPT_KEYWORDS.
  TODO: re-confirm against the clean post-1000-pkg matrix once the
  in-flight run completes (note: 218 of 233 post-KW entries had
  contaminated emerge data due to the @NPROC@ deploy mistake; have
  patched in-place but re-run the contaminated subset for clean
  comparison numbers).
  Originals (kept for traceability):
  - Investigate why emerge plan-fails on www-servers/nginx and
    media-gfx/graphviz while portage-ng plans both with assumptions.
    Decide whether portage-ng should also reject (or whether emerge
    is too strict here). The 2026-05-09 pretend tier added 7 more
    such cases: dev-util/{strace,ltrace,cmake,ninja,meson},
    sys-apps/tree, sys-apps/at, app-text/pandoc-cli, app-doc/doxygen.
  - net-irc/irssi: portage-ng pulls in dev-lang/perl-5.42.2 even
    though perl is part of stage3. Likely a vartree-vs-VDB lookup
    difference in build-time-only dep handling. Trace through
    prover/vdb.
  - net-irc/irssi: portage-ng selects irssi-1.4.5-r2, emerge picks
    irssi-1.4.5. Check preference predicate / version-selection
    logic.
  - VDB-delta packages where portage-ng pulls in extra perl/python
    deps that stage3 already provides: app-misc/mc (+4),
    net-analyzer/iftop (+3), net-dns/dnsmasq (+3), dev-vcs/mercurial
    (+4), media-libs/freetype (+9). Same root cause as irssi-perl
    above.


Bugs (evidence-based, found via tinderbox-ng compare)
=====================================================

- [FIXED 2026-05-09] pipeline silent-failure: when a sub-step (build
  or install of a dep) fails, the failure does not always propagate
  to the top-level pipeline exit code. 12/65 build-tier runs in the
  2026-05-09 matrix reported `exit 0` while the target was never
  merged into VDB. Fix:
    * Source/Pipeline/builder.pl asserts builder:last_build_status/3
      after every build/build_resume run.
    * Source/Application/Interface/Action/build.pl now calls
      action:maybe_ci_exit_on_build_failure/1 in --ci mode and
      halts with exit code 3 when Failed > 0.
  After-fix matrix (compare-matrix-20260509T150251): 0/65 silent
  failures.

- [FIXED 2026-05-09] planner over-decrement (root cause of all
  portage-ng-only build losses): prover:add_trigger stored FULL heads
  with their ?{Ctx} suffix in the trigger map's dependent lists. When
  the same canonical head was proven/re-proven with multiple contexts
  (e.g. self={mc} vs self={libICE}), it ended up in the dependent
  list multiple times, and the planner's decrement_and_enqueue then
  decremented the canonical depcount once per ctx-variant. Symptom:
  libICE:install (depcount=7) was scheduled in the SAME wave as its
  prerequisite xorg-proto:install when proving app-misc/mc, instead
  of the wave AFTER it. Fix: dedupe trigger dependents by canonical
  head in Source/Pipeline/prover.pl. After-fix matrix:
    * mc, dnsmasq, iftop, w3m, subversion ALL build to OK end-to-end
    * 54/65 BOTH_OK, 9/65 BOTH_FAIL (upstream issues, emerge also
      fails), 2/65 PN-only FAIL (download bug, see below)

- [FIXED 2026-05-09 commit 329a228c] Class A: SWI-Prolog
  $tmp_file_stream/4 throws existence_error(temporary_file, '') in
  download:fetch_layout_conf/2 under high parallelism (jed/lighttpd
  in the 65-pkg matrix; libX11/libXdmcp/libXt cascade-skipped 12 of
  22 portage-ng-only failures in the 1000-pkg matrix). Root cause was
  a race between concurrent download workers in the same process all
  hitting the cache-miss branch of download:mirror_layout/1 and each
  spawning their own tmp_file_stream-backed curl pipeline. Fix:
    * Source/Pipeline/Builder/download.pl: replaced tmp_file_stream
      with stdout(pipe(Out)) + read_string (no tmp file lifecycle).
    * Wrapped the cache-miss branch of download:mirror_layout/1 in
      with_mutex(download_mirror_layout, ...).
    * Source/Pipeline/builder.pl: defense-in-depth safe_mirror_layout
      wrapper falls back to flat layout on any unexpected exception.
  Verified on app-emacs/corfu (previously failing on this bug): no
  more tmp_file_stream errors, download cascade completes cleanly.

- [FIXED 2026-05-09 commit 162f2fde] Class B: fallback:env('USE',...)
  hardcoded 129-flag list (alsa, cairo, dbus, gif, gpm, harfbuzz,
  jpeg, png, sqlite, tiff, ...) was leaking into chroots even when
  config:portage_confdir/1 was set, as long as /etc/portage/make.conf
  omitted a USE= line. Tinderbox baseline make.conf intentionally has
  no USE=, so every chroot silently inherited the dev-machine flag
  set, making portage-ng pull unrelated cascades (alsa-lib + alsa-*
  for app-emacs/chess where emerge built emacs with -alsa). Fix:
  preference:getenv/2 skips fallback:env/2 when portage_confdir is
  configured. chess plan dropped 74 -> 28 actions; 0 portage-ng-only
  failures in the post-fix slice of the 1000-pkg matrix.

- [FIXED 2026-05-09 commit 29c1c223] Class C precursor: tinderbox
  baseline had no ACCEPT_KEYWORDS, so after the Class B fix removed
  the dev-fallback ~amd64, portage-ng had no keyword filter and
  selected live 9999 ebuilds (emacs-32.0.9999, java-config-9999)
  over emerge's stable picks. Fix: ACCEPT_KEYWORDS="~amd64" in
  Source/Application/Wrapper/Linux/tinderbox-ng.d/baseline.make.conf.

- [FIXED 2026-05-09 commit 08b6759f] mirror layout.conf was fetched
  once per process, which meant tinderbox-ng compare matrices (one
  fresh portage-ng-dev per session) issued ~1000 throwaway curls per
  full run AND degraded sessions to `flat` whenever the mirror
  flapped. Added on-disk cache under <distdir>/.mirror-layout.cache
  (24h TTL via config:mirror_layout_cache_ttl/1, only successful
  fetches are persisted, atomic write via tmp+rename). Verified
  inside chroot: 150ms cold -> 0ms warm-disk-hit on fresh process.

- [FIXED 2026-05-09] tinderbox-ng baseline-config deploys were
  unsafe: scp'ing
  Source/Application/Wrapper/Linux/tinderbox-ng.d/baseline.make.conf
  directly to /srv/tinderbox-ng/baseline/etc/portage/make.conf
  bypassed the @NPROC@ / @GENTOO_PROFILE@ substitution that
  cp_template() does at bootstrap time, leaving the file with literal
  "@NPROC@" placeholders that crash emerge with
  `Invalid --jobs parameter: '@NPROC@'`. Hit during the 1000-pkg
  matrix on 2026-05-09 and contaminated 218 of 233 post-KW entries
  (patched in-place with sed; new helper used for all subsequent
  deploys). Fix:
    * Source/Application/Wrapper/Linux/tinderbox-ng.d/deploy-baseline.sh
      applies the same substitutions as cp_template (NPROC resolved
      via ssh-to-remote so it matches the deploy target, GENTOO_PROFILE
      and PORTAGE_NG_ROOT from env or matching defaults) and refuses
      to deploy if any @TOKEN@ remains unresolved.
    * Source/Application/Wrapper/Linux/README.md gained an explicit
      "do not raw-scp a template" warning pointing at the helper.

- [FIXED 2026-05-09 commit ade8318c] Class G: race-condition in the
  shared distfiles bind mount. portage-ng and emerge running side by
  side in tinderbox-ng compare both wrote directly to DestPath and,
  on verification failure, deleted DestPath -- which could nuke the
  other process's still-incomplete (or freshly-finished) download.
  Symptom: dev-libs/cusparselt portage-ng-only failure even though
  the file was already on disk. A second cusparselt-specific symptom
  uncovered during the fix: download:upstream_url/4 used
  kb:query(src_uri(uri(...))) which only matched bare uri/3 terms
  and silently skipped USE-conditional SRC_URI, so packages whose
  download is gated by `amd64?` (cusparselt's NVIDIA tarball) had no
  upstream fallback when the Gentoo mirror returned 404. Fix:
    * Source/Pipeline/Builder/download.pl + Source/Pipeline/builder.pl:
      stage every download into DestPath.<pid>.<tid>.partial, verify
      size+hash on the temp, then atomic-rename to DestPath. On
      failure, only the temp is deleted; race_recover/3 checks
      whether another process landed a valid DestPath while we were
      curling, before failing the job.
    * download:upstream_url/4 now pulls all(src_uri(Model)) and
      query:deep_member(preference, uri(Proto, Base, File), Model)
      to traverse use_conditional_group / any_of_group structures so
      USE-gated upstream URIs resolve correctly under the active
      profile USE.
  Verified: cusparselt rebuild from scratch -> mirror 404 -> NVIDIA
  upstream URL via deep_member traversal -> install OK in both pn
  and emerge sessions.

- [FIXED 2026-05-10] Class F: any_of_group ranking ignored profile
  USE_EXPAND selection. For sec-keys/openpgp-keys-gentoo-developers
  the BDEPEND has a 4-branch ||(...) over python:3.14, python:3.13,
  python:3.12, python:3.11 (each branch also requires
  python-gnupg[python_targets_python3_X(-)?]). The profile sets
  PYTHON_TARGETS="python3_13", but candidate:dep_rank scored every
  branch the same (Pref=0 because python-gnupg isn't installed in
  any branch), so candidate:prioritize_deps_keep_all fell back to
  left-to-right order and picked python:3.14. That cascaded into a
  python-gnupg rebuild with python_targets_python3_14, which in
  turn pulled rebuilds of setuptools, gpep517 and friends with the
  same wrong target -- 12 actions vs emerge's 3.
  Fix: Source/Domain/Gentoo/Rules/candidate.pl
    * dep_use_expand_profile_score/2 sums +1 / -1 per USE_EXPAND
      USE-dep depending on whether preference:global_use/1 agrees
      with the profile (recurses into all_of_group / any_of_group
      / use_conditional_group; non-USE_EXPAND USE-deps contribute 0).
    * prioritize_deps_keep_all keysort key extended:
      NegLicOk-NegRank-NegOverlap-NegSnap-NegUEScore-I-Dep
      (UE_EXPAND match wins over left-to-right order, loses to all
      existing stronger signals).
  Verified: sec-keys/openpgp-keys-gentoo-developers plan 12 -> 3
  actions matching emerge; smoke set (python-gnupg, setuptools,
  gpep517, glibc, openssl, git, openssh, glib, geneweb, chess,
  portage) all unchanged.

- [FIXED 2026-05-10] tinderbox-ng deploy automation gap.
  Concern: many improvements (NPROC substitution, ccache install + chown,
  /etc/ccache.conf max_size, sessions tmpfs, ACCEPT_KEYWORDS, orphan
  reaper, refresh-portage-ng) accumulated incrementally; risk of a fresh
  VM deploy missing one of them silently.
  Fix: bootstrap path now fully scripted end-to-end and audited.
    - new `cmd_doctor`: aggregates every host-side prerequisite (bash 5+,
      required + optional bins, kernel overlay + namespace + devtmpfs,
      template files, portage-ng source pointer, disk space floor, tmpfs
      vs RAM, mDNS resolution) and reports all issues in one pass. Run
      automatically by `cmd_bootstrap` (skip with TINDERBOX_SKIP_DOCTOR=1).
    - new `cmd_selftest`: runs `compare --pretend $TINDERBOX_SELFTEST_TARGET`
      (default sys-apps/portage) in a throwaway session to confirm the
      baseline is wired correctly. Auto-runs at end of bootstrap when
      TINDERBOX_BOOTSTRAP_SELFTEST=1.
    - new `Source/Application/Wrapper/Linux/deploy-host.sh`: one-shot
      host install (rsync of script + templates -> symlink -> doctor ->
      optional bootstrap -> optional selftest). Forwards the relevant
      env (CCACHE_MAX_SIZE, SESSIONS_TMPFS_SIZE, STAGE3_*, GENTOO_PROFILE,
      etc.) so a fresh VM goes from clean to ready with one command:
        TINDERBOX_BOOTSTRAP_SELFTEST=1 \
          ./Source/Application/Wrapper/Linux/deploy-host.sh \
              --bootstrap root@vm-linux.local
    - README.md updated: lists deploy-host.sh, documents doctor +
      selftest subcommands, adds the new env vars to the reference table,
      and shows the recommended one-shot deploy alongside the manual
      equivalent.

- [INVESTIGATED 2026-05-10; not a planner gap] Initial review of the
  46 `FAIL/FAIL` rows from compare-matrix-20260510T143057 suggested 19
  cases (15 EM_NEED_USE_DEP + 3 EM_REQUIRED_USE + 1 EM_MASKED) might
  expose a USE-dep / REQUIRED_USE learning gap in PN. Spot-checking
  app-admin/keepass (RDEPEND=dev-dotnet/libgdiplus[cairo], libgdiplus
  ships cairo IUSE bare = default-disabled, emerge refuses to auto-flip)
  showed PN actually plans this correctly:
    Step 1 : useflag dev-dotnet/libgdiplus-6.2 (cairo)
    Step 23: install dev-dotnet/libgdiplus-6.2 (USE modified)
             USE = "cairo"
    >>> USE flag change (2 packages):
        dev-dotnet/libgdiplus cairo
        x11-libs/cairo X
  Verified the wiring end-to-end:
    * candidate.pl:3104-3129 grouped_dep_tag_suggestions tags the child
      Ctx with `suggestion(use_change, Repo://Entry, Changes)` whenever
      `use:build_with_use_changes` finds an IUSE flag that needs flipping.
    * use.pl:308-329 build_with_use_changes only emits use_change(F,
      enable|disable) when the flag is in IUSE *and* the candidate's
      effective state currently differs.
    * ebuild_exec.pl:291-316 apply_ctx_use_overrides reads
      `suggestion(use_change, _, Changes)` from Ctx and applies it on top
      of the KB-derived USE assoc *before* the ebuild runs, so the build
      sees the corrected USE.
    * builder.pl:1380-1478 dispatch_suggestions writes the same change
      to /etc/portage/package.use/00portage-ng-auto under
      with_mutex(build_auto_config), then refreshes userconfig so the
      next ebuild in the wave starts from the persisted state.
  The actual reason these 19 rows show up as FAIL/FAIL is that PN's
  build dies on UNRELATED earlier deps (Class H env failures - see next
  entry). Conclusion: NO planner work needed; the planning side is
  already a PN-only win. Once Class H is fixed, the FAIL/FAIL count
  should drop sharply and these 19 should reclassify as ok/fail
  (PN-only wins). No code change in this commit; documented here so
  the next reviewer doesn't redo the same investigation.

- [FIXED 2026-05-10] Class H autotools / eautoreconf chroot-environment
  failure was the dominant blocker for the FAIL/FAIL bucket. Root cause
  was *NOT* the autotools eclass (no upstream patch needed) and *NOT* an
  overlay/chroot quirk - it was tinderbox-ng's own `bootstrap_freeze`
  using `chmod -R a-w "$BASELINE_DIR"` which stripped the owner-write bit
  from every file in the baseline (gnuconfig data files dropped from
  0755 to 0555). Sessions then copied those 0555 files into the work
  tree via libtoolize, and the autotools eclass `cp ... .` could not
  overwrite them. Fixes shipped:
    1. `bootstrap_freeze` now does `find -type d -exec chmod a-w` only.
       Directories give the same defense against accidental host writes;
       file perms remain whatever the package manager set them to. The
       previous comment about overlay copy-up "creating a fresh file with
       default permissions" was factually wrong (overlayfs copy-up
       preserves lower-layer mode bit-for-bit, and bare cp / install -p
       preserve source mode). Both observations corrected in code
       comments at bootstrap_freeze.
    2. New `tinderbox-ng baseline-repair-perms` subcommand re-emerges
       the canonical victims (currently sys-devel/gnuconfig) through the
       normal Portage install path so existing baselines can be migrated
       without a full re-bootstrap. Extensible: pass extra atoms as
       additional victims surface.
    3. New doctor check (`baseline gnuconfig perms`): warns when the
       canary files are not owner-writable and points to
       `baseline-repair-perms`.
  Verified end-to-end on vm-linux baseline 2026-05-10:
    * libev install: was failing with `cp: cannot create regular file
      './config.guess': Permission denied`; now installs cleanly in ~15s.
    * libcap-ng install: ~20s, clean.
    * colm install: ~3min, clean (longest of the autotools-heavy victims).
  All three were in the top-5 of the previous matrix's FAIL/FAIL count
  (126 freetype + 85 colm + 43 libcap-ng + ... + 17 libev). The
  all-packages matrix run was aborted, baseline repaired, and resumed
  via `tinderbox-ng continue --background` - expectation is the
  FAIL/FAIL bucket drops sharply and several of the previously-flagged
  PN-only-on-planning wins (cf. previous todo entry) reclassify as
  ok/fail.
  Top failing build targets in compare-matrix-20260510T143057 are all
  environmental:
       126 media-libs/freetype-2.14.3
        85 dev-util/colm-0.14.7-r4
        43 sys-libs/libcap-ng-0.9.3
        34 app-text/aspell-0.60.8.2
        31 media-libs/alsa-lib-1.2.15.3
        30 dev-lang/ghc-9.2.8
        22 media-libs/audiofile-0.3.6-r6
        17 dev-libs/libev-4.33
        ... + a long tail of dev-perl/* modules
  Reproducer: `tinderbox-ng new tmp; tinderbox-ng exec tmp -- emerge -1
  dev-libs/libev` in a fresh chroot fails identically on PN AND emerge:
       * Running 'libtoolize --install --copy --force --automake' ...  ok
       * Running 'aclocal --system-acdir=...' ...                      ok
       cp: cannot create regular file './config.guess': Permission denied
       cp: cannot create regular file './config.sub':   Permission denied
       * ERROR: dev-libs/libev-4.33::gentoo failed (prepare phase)
       *   (no error message)
       *   environment, line 2151:  Called eautoreconf
       *   environment, line  970:  Called eautoconf '--force'
       *   environment, line  882:  Called die
       *   The specific snippet of code:
       *       cp "${_gnuconfig}"/config.{guess,sub} . || die;
  Root cause: `libtoolize --install --copy --force` (run earlier in the
  same eautoreconf chain) installs config.{guess,sub} into the work tree
  with mode 0555 (matching its source files in /usr/share/libtool/build-
  aux/). The subsequent `cp "${_gnuconfig}"/config.{guess,sub} .` from
  the autotools eclass uses a bare `cp` (no `-f`, no `chmod +w` first),
  which cannot overwrite the read-only files. Both portage-ng and emerge
  hit it because both invoke the same eclass under userpriv.
  Affects both engines equally - this is NOT a portage-ng regression -
  but it inflates the FAIL/FAIL bucket and obscures legitimate PN-only
  wins (see previous entry).
  Fix landed: dirs-only freeze + baseline-repair-perms (see "Fixes
  shipped" block above). No upstream eclass patch required; the eclass
  bare `cp` is benign on a correctly-permissioned baseline.

Upstream / ebuild bugs hitting both engines (compare-matrix collateral)
=======================================================================

Tracking real upstream Gentoo / package-Makefile bugs that show up as
FAIL/FAIL in the compare matrix. Both portage-ng and emerge hit them
identically (verified: same failure in a fresh `tinderbox-ng new` +
`tinderbox-ng exec -- emerge -1 <pkg>`). NOT planner regressions; useful
to batch as upstream Gentoo bug reports later.

- [BOTH] dev-lang/yasm-1.3.0-r2 - re2c segfault during compile.
  Failure: `make: *** [Makefile:4158: nasm-token.c] Segmentation fault`
  after the freshly-built `re2c` tool runs on `nasm-token.re`. yasm
  is unmaintained (last release 2014); almost certainly an issue
  with re2c's generated state machine miscompiled by modern GCC.
  Cascades to: app-arch/p7zip and any other yasm consumer.

- [BOTH] sys-block/mtx-1.3.12-r1 - sandbox violation in install phase.
  Failure: `install -c -m 755 -d /usr/share/man <D>/usr/share/man/man1`
  (Makefile passes a bare `/usr/share/man` without `$(DESTDIR)` prefix
  alongside the correctly-prefixed manpage dir) -> `ACCESS DENIED:
  fchmod: /usr/share/man` -> `make: *** [Makefile:114: install]
  Segmentation fault`. Real upstream Makefile bug; mtx is largely
  unmaintained (last release 2017). Cascades to: app-backup/bacula
  and any other tape-library consumer.

- [BOTH] app-arch/stuffit-5.2.0.611-r1 - unfetchable + masked.
  Masked by `no-multilib/package.mask` (i386 binary) AND
  `RESTRICT="fetch"` (defunct Aladdin download URL). PN's auto-unmask
  fires correctly (PN-only planning win) but no engine can fetch the
  proprietary tarball. Legitimate "can't be installed" - leave masked.

PN-side latent gaps (not Class H, not regressions; future work)
================================================================

- [PN-GAP] Soft blocker `![cat/pkg[useflag]]` against an installed
  package whose USE includes `useflag` is not detected at planning.
  Example: `app-arch/hardlink` has
  `RDEPEND="!>=sys-apps/util-linux-2.34[hardlink]"`.
  Our baseline has util-linux-2.41.3 with `hardlink` USE built in
  (system installs `/usr/bin/hardlink` from util-linux). Emerge
  correctly errors out at planning ("packages which cannot be
  installed at the same time"); PN plans the install and fails at
  the merge step (file collision). Worth handling in PN's blocker
  resolver: when a `![cpv[use]]` blocker matches an installed package
  with the listed USE flag enabled, surface it at planning time
  (either as a domain assumption "would need to disable USE=hardlink
  on util-linux", or as a hard refusal). Single-package impact in the
  current matrix, but the pattern is general and worth catching.

Open
====
